if you ever saw a wooden surfboard it  was probably hanging in somebody's house  or underneath the deck but kind of in  the last 10 or 15 years Wood has made a  big comeback they surf wonderfully  that's beautiful it feels great to be on  I always had a love for wood and kind of  passionate about things that went in the  water I've kind of been around wooden  boats most of my life there's just so  much character the way they're built the  stories that come with a lot of that has  translated over to why we build  surfboards the way we do well we first  started thinking about building  surfboards Wood was the natural material  we chose they have a very high strength  to weight ratio wooden surfboards have  the longest history of use in the surf  world than any other material people  have been surfing wooden surfboards for  since kind of the first documented  history of people riding waves board  starts in CAD where we take a 3d model  break down the shape and kind of create  frames and templates and all the inner  parts of the board that we get cut  [Music]  we take those frames and we just pop  them out assemble the frame into kind of  a skeleton we go out to our lumber shed  pick a bunch of cedar and bring them  into our milk shop put glue on the edges  clamp it all together to a panel  everything's booked match you have  symmetry and the colors and texture of  the wood cutting your outline take our  assembled frame gently put it down right  on top of the plane once that foundation  is started you're basically building up  the three-dimensional shape the outside  part of the board using lots of strips  that kind of interlock and fit together  every piece of wood is gonna be  different and it's all going to react  differently there are frustrations with  it but I think that's one of the things  that keep the challenging you learn to  read the kind of what the grain lines  are doing and what the color of the wood  is telling you once those rails are  cleaned up we put our top planks on it's  a little bit like putting a lid on a box  it's like this is it whatever it's  inside that board is staying inside that  by tensioning some rope you can clamp  the two together it's like a time  capsule what makes our board unique are  they are built right here in York  they're built using material that grows  right here in the state of Maine they're  built 100% by hand by local craftspeople  and surfers there's a lot of time that  goes into these boards between 40 and 60  hours to finish we wanted to build  surfboards that were as hollow as they  could be but still be strong enough to  work well too late and they don't surf  well but too heavy and they don't surf  well so there's a balance point there  and that's where we try to be taking the  board off the rocker table once the top  has gone on that is by far one of my top  favorites parts it's just you and a  shaping stand a board and a hand plane  everything else is out of your view it's  simple it's pleasurable it's quiet you  could be with your thoughts and you can  just kind of be present there's  something about taking a nice sharp edge  tool to a beautiful piece of wood and  feeling the curls coming off and knowing  that every pass that you're making  you're kind of getting it closer and  closer to what you have in mind once  we've shaped it down and the board's  looking like it's supposed to look like  transferred to go into the glass and 1/4  ounce fiberglass laying it over the  board draping it over the edges cutting  it applying epoxy and on the board  once both sides are Lam coated it's time  to put in the hardware drill and router  and install the feed box and on the  other side we drill and put in the lease  plug and event there's a lot of error  just naturally inside the board and that  air wants to expand and contract with  temperature changes and that vent has a  little piece of gore-tex fabric in it  and it allows air to kind of breathe  both ways but it doesn't want water once  all that Hardware is put in goes back  into the glassing room we brush a nice  beautiful thick coat of epoxy over the  whole surface and that we call a gloss  coat that's supposed to look beautiful  and glossy and shiny and flawless you  get to see that board come to life  whatever the colors in the wood they  really come out it just makes it look  like candy you just want to touch it and  run your hands down it and it's just  kind of icing on the cake  once everything is hard we put it into  what we call the oven get that epoxy to  cure really kind of bake it and just get  it to harden put in the fin screw in the  vent then ship it out we just love the  idea of building something that's  sustainable and long-lasting and made by  hand  so it gives you a deeper connection to  the product over something you might buy  on a shelf  [Music]  when a customer gets the board they feel  it they a hundred percent feel the  amount of work and the amount of passion  that went into the board so I'm not  really looking at hours and efficiency  and looking at like doing it right and  enjoying it as we go  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you  [Music]  [Laughter]  you
 

